1
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,300
Many of the materials we work with in our labs

2
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:41,620
give off fumes, mists, vapors, particulates,

3
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:43,860
or aerosols that are hazardous.

4
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:46,100
To minimize exposure to these materials,

5
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,980
we need to take special precautions.

6
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:51,820
Often, this means working within a hood.

7
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:55,900
We can get an idea of how to work safely with hoods

8
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:58,740
by looking at one of the most frequently used types,

9
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:14,080
the chemical exhaust hood.

10
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:16,800
Exhaust hoods protect us by preventing contaminants

11
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,080
within the hood from entering our breathing zone.

12
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:22,360
A protective barrier is created by air

13
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:25,320
that's pulled into and through the hood.

14
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:28,800
This inward airflow helps keep hazardous fumes and vapors

15
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:33,120
from escaping and reaching anyone outside the hood.

16
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,400
Contaminants that are captured within the airflow

17
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:37,880
are filtered, diluted, and exhausted

18
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:41,350
through the hood's duct system.

19
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:43,790
This air is then sent outside the building,

20
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:45,670
where the contaminants are further diluted

21
00:01:45,670 --> 00:01:48,780
into the atmosphere.

22
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:50,580
Hoods also protect the operator

23
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:52,620
from other physical threats.

24
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:54,980
The sash provides protection from hazards

25
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:57,140
like chemical splashes and sprays,

26
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:59,980
as well as fires and minor explosions.

27
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:02,060
Generally, you want to pull the sash down

28
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:03,340
as far as possible,

29
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:04,700
while keeping it at a level

30
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,500
that allows you to work comfortably.

31
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:10,500
Many hood manufacturers have determined

32
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:12,700
the levels at which the sashes in their hoods

33
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:15,180
should be placed for maximum protection,

34
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:17,420
so you'll want to follow their recommendations

35
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:21,180
if at all possible.

36
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:22,620
When working within a hood,

37
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:25,380
don't forget to wear the personal protective equipment

38
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:28,260
that the materials you're using call for.

39
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:30,060
Even with a hood's protection,

40
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:32,580
you need to wear at least the safety eyewear,

41
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:37,100
lab coat, and gloves that you normally use.

42
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:40,420
You should also read the substance's safety data sheets.

43
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:42,780
These will tell you each substance's ingredients,

44
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:45,620
properties, and hazards.

45
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:47,380
And if the substances you're working with

46
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:49,220
require additional protection,

47
00:02:49,220 --> 00:03:04,660
you should use it as well.

48
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:06,740
To ensure that it's operating safely,

49
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:08,620
each lab hood is thoroughly tested

50
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:10,060
when it's first installed,

51
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:12,820
whenever there's a change in the ventilation system

52
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:15,900
and on a regular basis thereafter.

53
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,140
There are specific steps that need to be taken

54
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:22,740
to determine if a hood is operating the way it should.

55
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:26,460
First, the air circulation around the hood should be checked.

56
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:29,140
Your supervisor or a qualified engineer

57
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:30,420
will measure the flow of air

58
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:32,780
six inches in front of the hood.

59
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:36,020
This cross-draft should be monitored carefully.

60
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,500
Drafts greater than 20 linear feet per minute

61
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:41,260
will adversely affect the performance of the hood.

62
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:45,340
Next, a smoke tube should be used

63
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:46,940
to make sure that the air is flowing

64
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,220
as it should inside the hood.

65
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:51,420
If the smoke heads for the ventilation ducts,

66
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:55,640
then the hood is operating correctly.

67
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:57,400
It's then time to open the sash

68
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,560
and measure the rate of air

69
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:00,760
coming through the face of the hood.

70
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:06,190
This measurement is known as the hood's face velocity.

71
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:08,590
An accurate measurement of the face velocity

72
00:04:08,590 --> 00:04:10,230
is very important,

73
00:04:10,230 --> 00:04:12,550
so a precision instrument like an anemometer

74
00:04:12,550 --> 00:04:14,430
or a velometer should be used.

75
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:17,990
Never try to use something like sheets of tissue paper

76
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:20,550
to give you an indication of face velocity.

77
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:25,920
They will only show you whether the hood is on or off.

78
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:27,840
When measuring face velocity,

79
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:29,400
the sash should be adjusted

80
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:31,280
as if you were about to begin work.

81
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:34,400
Your supervisor will divide the hood face

82
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:35,600
into a grid pattern

83
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:39,000
and measure the velocity of the air in each section.

84
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,280
Values at specific points may vary

85
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:43,440
by plus or minus 25%

86
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:46,480
without compromising the safety of the hood's operation.

87
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:49,600
But no measurement should be below

88
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,680
60 linear feet per minute.

89
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:53,920
If your test shows a lower speed,

90
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,240
the airflow should be adjusted

91
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:57,040
before the hood is used again.

92
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,520
Your supervisor will also compare

93
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:03,360
the face velocity of the air coming through the hood

94
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:05,320
with the speed of the cross draft

95
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:06,880
to make sure that the cross draft

96
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:10,520
is never greater than 20% of the face velocity.

97
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:11,680
If this is a problem,

98
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:14,040
the interior hood baffle will be adjusted

99
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:15,240
or if necessary,

100
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,200
the ventilation of the laboratory itself

101
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:18,600
may need to be altered.

102
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:22,240
In general, hoods will bring in air

103
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:23,480
at a face velocity

104
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:26,520
of about 80 to 100 linear feet per minute,

105
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:28,800
but you may need to use a higher velocity

106
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:31,200
for some of the work that you do.

107
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:34,360
A velocity of about 125 linear feet per minute

108
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:37,320
may be required for some procedures.

109
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:38,520
Be careful, though.

110
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:40,720
Higher velocities can create turbulence

111
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:42,000
both inside a hood

112
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,880
and at the front of the hood face.

113
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:46,640
This can cause vapors within the hood

114
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:48,840
to spill out into the laboratory,

115
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,680
putting you and your coworkers at risk.

116
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:53,520
In general, face velocity

117
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:57,520
should not exceed 150 linear feet per minute.

118
00:05:57,520 --> 00:05:58,960
And remember, you should have

119
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:00,600
the face velocity of your hoods

120
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:05,560
measured regularly once a year at minimum.

121
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,800
Once it's determined that the air velocities

122
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:08,800
in and around the hood

123
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:10,400
are within accepted ranges,

124
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:11,840
it's important to make sure the hood

125
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:14,640
is relatively free of air turbulence.

126
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:15,840
Turbulence can be checked

127
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:18,840
by observing smoke patterns within the hood.

128
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,400
If there's excessive turbulence

129
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:22,560
or if the hood fails to capture the smoke,

130
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:24,800
adjustments will be required.

131
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:26,600
The engineer who is working on the hood

132
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:28,640
will consider changing a number of things,

133
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:32,240
such as the location of equipment within the hood,

134
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:34,160
the hood's face velocity,

135
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:36,680
the location of air input ports,

136
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:38,760
the physical location of the hood,

137
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:39,880
and the volume of air

138
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:43,390
being brought into the hood.

139
00:06:43,390 --> 00:06:44,430
As you can see,

140
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:46,510
there is a lot that goes into making sure

141
00:06:46,510 --> 00:06:48,470
that a hood is functioning properly.

142
00:06:48,470 --> 00:06:50,110
If you suspect that your hood

143
00:06:50,110 --> 00:06:51,750
isn't performing as it should,

144
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:53,030
talk to your supervisor

145
00:06:53,030 --> 00:07:09,470
to determine if a reevaluation is needed.

146
00:07:09,470 --> 00:07:11,470
Having a lab hood set up correctly

147
00:07:11,470 --> 00:07:13,430
is just half the battle.

148
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:15,110
In order for hoods to be effective,

149
00:07:15,110 --> 00:07:18,070
we've also got to use them correctly.

150
00:07:18,070 --> 00:07:19,910
This means doing everything we can

151
00:07:19,910 --> 00:07:22,990
to maintain proper airflow within the hood.

152
00:07:24,150 --> 00:07:25,030
To begin with,

153
00:07:25,030 --> 00:07:26,790
the work you do should be performed

154
00:07:26,790 --> 00:07:29,590
at least six inches inside the hood.

155
00:07:29,590 --> 00:07:31,590
This enables the airflow within the hood

156
00:07:31,590 --> 00:07:33,470
to capture any dangerous vapors

157
00:07:33,470 --> 00:07:35,270
and particles near the face

158
00:07:35,270 --> 00:07:36,870
and prevent them from escaping

159
00:07:36,870 --> 00:07:38,390
into your breathing zone.

160
00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:41,190
Since large pieces of equipment

161
00:07:41,190 --> 00:07:42,390
such as orbital shakers

162
00:07:42,390 --> 00:07:44,390
can obstruct the airflow within a hood,

163
00:07:44,390 --> 00:07:45,510
they should be elevated

164
00:07:45,510 --> 00:07:47,870
to allow air to pass under them.

165
00:07:49,150 --> 00:07:51,590
Never use hoods as storage cabinets.

166
00:07:51,590 --> 00:07:53,870
Overloading a hood restricts its airflow

167
00:07:53,870 --> 00:07:55,910
and can result in the dangerous buildup

168
00:07:55,910 --> 00:07:57,990
of hazardous vapors.

169
00:07:57,990 --> 00:08:01,470
Additionally, chemicals that are stored

170
00:08:01,470 --> 00:08:03,270
inside a hood can make an incident

171
00:08:03,270 --> 00:08:06,470
such as a spill or fire even worse.

172
00:08:06,470 --> 00:08:09,070
So if you're not actively working with a chemical,

173
00:08:09,190 --> 00:08:11,710
put it in its normal storage space.

174
00:08:12,710 --> 00:08:14,630
You should also keep the sash closed

175
00:08:14,630 --> 00:08:15,750
as much as possible

176
00:08:15,750 --> 00:08:17,590
when you're not working in your hood.

177
00:08:17,590 --> 00:08:18,550
Remember to consult

178
00:08:18,550 --> 00:08:20,710
the manufacturer's recommendations.

179
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:24,390
And pay attention to the hood's own air monitors.

180
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:25,790
They'll warn you when the airflow

181
00:08:25,790 --> 00:08:27,270
through the hood isn't strong enough

182
00:08:27,270 --> 00:08:31,430
to contain the hazards within it.

183
00:08:31,430 --> 00:08:32,790
It's important to be careful

184
00:08:32,790 --> 00:08:34,190
about how you move

185
00:08:34,190 --> 00:08:36,750
when you're in front of a hood as well.

186
00:08:36,750 --> 00:08:38,110
Even face velocities

187
00:08:38,110 --> 00:08:39,790
of 100 linear feet per minute

188
00:08:39,990 --> 00:08:41,350
can easily be disrupted

189
00:08:41,350 --> 00:08:42,950
by rapidly removing your arms

190
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:44,870
from inside a hood.

191
00:08:44,870 --> 00:08:46,950
Even just walking by the hood face

192
00:08:46,950 --> 00:08:48,910
can cause problems.

193
00:08:48,910 --> 00:08:50,190
So you need to be careful

194
00:08:50,190 --> 00:08:52,110
of any physical movements you make

195
00:08:52,110 --> 00:08:54,190
that might alter a hood's airflow.

196
00:08:55,110 --> 00:08:56,870
After all, most hoods aren't even

197
00:08:56,870 --> 00:08:58,670
as powerful as a vacuum cleaner.

198
00:08:59,670 --> 00:09:01,350
Another thing you need to guard against

199
00:09:01,350 --> 00:09:03,710
is solid objects such as papers

200
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:05,750
entering the exhaust ducts of a hood.

201
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:07,190
If they lodge in the ducts

202
00:09:07,190 --> 00:09:08,510
or the hood's fans,

203
00:09:08,510 --> 00:09:10,310
it will significantly degrade

204
00:09:10,310 --> 00:09:12,030
the effectiveness of the hood.

205
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:15,390
And never place your head

206
00:09:15,390 --> 00:09:17,270
inside an exhaust hood.

207
00:09:17,270 --> 00:09:19,630
Not only will it disrupt the airflow,

208
00:09:19,630 --> 00:09:20,590
you could be overcome

209
00:09:20,590 --> 00:09:23,110
by hazardous fumes or vapors.

210
00:09:23,110 --> 00:09:24,630
If you need to make adjustments

211
00:09:24,630 --> 00:09:25,750
inside the hood,

212
00:09:25,750 --> 00:09:41,440
talk to your supervisor.

213
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:42,920
Most of the hoods we work with

214
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:44,000
are designed to keep us

215
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,680
from breathing in hazardous fumes

216
00:09:45,680 --> 00:09:46,760
and vapors.

217
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:48,320
However, there are also hoods

218
00:09:48,320 --> 00:09:49,440
that are built to take care

219
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:51,480
of hazardous particles.

220
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:53,400
Biohazard hoods, for example,

221
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:54,600
are designed to capture

222
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:57,360
toxic and infectious particulates.

223
00:09:57,360 --> 00:09:59,280
These types of hoods are most often

224
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:00,640
used with work involving

225
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:03,080
clinical specimens or body fluids.

226
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:05,720
The major difference

227
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:07,120
in these particulate cabinets

228
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:08,880
is their filtering systems.

229
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:10,480
Not only are these systems able

230
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:12,240
to capture very fine particles

231
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:13,080
and aerosols,

232
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:14,640
but they don't evacuate

233
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:16,880
these contaminants into the outside air.

234
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:19,920
The most effective of these systems

235
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:22,480
is the high-efficiency particulate air,

236
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:23,960
HEPA filter.

237
00:10:23,960 --> 00:10:25,400
A HEPA filter captures

238
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:26,640
particulates as small

239
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:28,480
as three-tenths of a micron

240
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:32,360
at a success rate of 99.99%.

241
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:33,280
Remember, though,

242
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:34,200
the HEPA filter

243
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:35,960
is a particulate filter.

244
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:38,200
It does not prevent hazardous gases

245
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:39,000
from passing through

246
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,880
the exhaust system.

247
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:42,200
If the substances you're working

248
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:43,840
with give off hazardous particles

249
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:45,160
and gases,

250
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:46,480
talk to your supervisor

251
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:47,760
about which hoods in your lab

252
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:52,020
will protect you from both hazards.

253
00:10:52,020 --> 00:10:53,180
There are hoods that provide

254
00:10:53,180 --> 00:10:54,140
protection against

255
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:56,340
other specialized hazards as well.

256
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:58,100
While you may not normally use them,

257
00:10:58,100 --> 00:10:59,980
you should know what they are there for

258
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:01,660
and how they work.

259
00:11:01,660 --> 00:11:02,940
The most common of these hoods

260
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:04,620
are used with perchloric acids

261
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:06,300
and radioisotopes.

262
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:08,940
The distinguishing feature

263
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:10,460
of a perchloric acid hood

264
00:11:10,460 --> 00:11:12,460
is its washdown capability.

265
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:14,060
This prevents the dangerous buildup

266
00:11:14,060 --> 00:11:15,620
of reactive residues.

267
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:17,860
One thing to remember,

268
00:11:17,860 --> 00:11:19,020
organic materials

269
00:11:19,020 --> 00:11:20,060
should never be used

270
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:22,260
in perchloric acid hoods.

271
00:11:22,260 --> 00:11:23,660
An explosive reaction

272
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:25,300
may result from the mixture

273
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:29,510
of organics and the acids residue.

274
00:11:29,510 --> 00:11:30,950
If you're working with material

275
00:11:30,950 --> 00:11:32,110
that is radioactive,

276
00:11:32,110 --> 00:11:33,070
you should be using

277
00:11:33,070 --> 00:11:35,030
a radioisotope hood.

278
00:11:35,030 --> 00:11:36,430
This hood is impermeable

279
00:11:36,430 --> 00:11:37,950
to radioactive material

280
00:11:37,950 --> 00:11:53,630
and will minimize dangerous exposure.

281
00:11:53,630 --> 00:11:54,950
No matter what type of hood

282
00:11:54,950 --> 00:11:55,590
you're using

283
00:11:55,590 --> 00:11:57,390
or how many precautions you take,

284
00:11:57,390 --> 00:11:59,590
things can still go wrong.

285
00:11:59,590 --> 00:12:01,510
So it's important to be prepared

286
00:12:01,510 --> 00:12:03,630
in the event a hood-related accident

287
00:12:03,630 --> 00:12:05,110
occurs in your lab.

288
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:08,030
Spills inside a hood

289
00:12:08,030 --> 00:12:10,270
need to be dealt with immediately.

290
00:12:10,270 --> 00:12:11,350
Follow your facility's

291
00:12:11,350 --> 00:12:12,870
cleanup procedures.

292
00:12:12,870 --> 00:12:13,910
Soak up the spill

293
00:12:13,910 --> 00:12:15,270
with an absorbent material

294
00:12:15,270 --> 00:12:16,950
and dispose of it properly.

295
00:12:18,430 --> 00:12:20,510
Before handling any substance,

296
00:12:20,510 --> 00:12:23,030
you should read its safety data sheet.

297
00:12:23,030 --> 00:12:24,710
Effective emergency response

298
00:12:24,710 --> 00:12:25,990
begins with your knowledge

299
00:12:25,990 --> 00:12:27,430
of the spilled material.

300
00:12:28,510 --> 00:12:30,070
Small fires in a hood

301
00:12:30,070 --> 00:12:30,830
can be put out

302
00:12:30,830 --> 00:12:31,830
with extinguishers

303
00:12:31,830 --> 00:12:33,350
or through suffocation

304
00:12:33,350 --> 00:12:34,190
by covering them

305
00:12:34,190 --> 00:12:35,150
with a watch glass

306
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:36,870
or inverted beaker.

307
00:12:36,870 --> 00:12:37,910
Some hoods even have

308
00:12:37,910 --> 00:12:40,270
their own fire suppression systems.

309
00:12:40,270 --> 00:12:41,710
With them, all you have to do

310
00:12:41,710 --> 00:12:42,910
is flick a switch.

311
00:12:44,110 --> 00:12:45,070
But in any case,

312
00:12:45,070 --> 00:12:46,030
you should know where

313
00:12:46,030 --> 00:12:47,390
the nearest fire extinguisher

314
00:12:47,390 --> 00:12:50,910
is in your lab.

315
00:12:50,910 --> 00:12:51,710
In the event of

316
00:12:51,710 --> 00:12:53,430
an uncontrollable fire,

317
00:12:53,430 --> 00:12:55,510
close the sash and get out.

318
00:12:55,510 --> 00:12:56,430
Sound the alarm

319
00:12:56,430 --> 00:13:00,870
and call the fire department.

320
00:13:00,870 --> 00:13:02,150
Ventilation failures

321
00:13:02,150 --> 00:13:03,670
are another type of emergency

322
00:13:03,670 --> 00:13:05,630
that you should be prepared for.

323
00:13:05,630 --> 00:13:06,510
A motor failure

324
00:13:06,510 --> 00:13:07,390
or malfunction

325
00:13:07,390 --> 00:13:08,390
in the electrical lines

326
00:13:08,390 --> 00:13:09,230
can bring your hood's

327
00:13:09,230 --> 00:13:10,230
ventilation system

328
00:13:10,230 --> 00:13:11,590
to a standstill,

329
00:13:11,590 --> 00:13:13,150
releasing harmful fumes,

330
00:13:13,150 --> 00:13:14,550
vapors and particles

331
00:13:14,550 --> 00:13:16,750
in the process.

332
00:13:16,750 --> 00:13:18,070
Talk to your supervisor

333
00:13:18,070 --> 00:13:19,270
about your facility's

334
00:13:19,270 --> 00:13:20,510
emergency plan

335
00:13:20,510 --> 00:13:21,830
so that you'll know exactly

336
00:13:21,830 --> 00:13:22,750
what to do in case

337
00:13:22,750 --> 00:13:23,910
of an equipment failure

338
00:13:23,910 --> 00:13:32,100
or other ventilation problem.

339
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:33,860
You can never be too careful

340
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:35,700
when using a laboratory hood.

341
00:13:35,700 --> 00:13:36,940
Remember, the reason

342
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:38,220
you're working under a hood

343
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:39,220
is that you're handling

344
00:13:39,220 --> 00:13:41,980
substances that are hazardous.

345
00:13:41,980 --> 00:13:45,390
Let's review.

346
00:13:45,390 --> 00:13:46,190
Make sure the hoods

347
00:13:46,190 --> 00:13:46,830
you work with

348
00:13:46,830 --> 00:13:48,030
are set up correctly

349
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:48,950
and that they meet

350
00:13:48,950 --> 00:13:52,310
all performance requirements.

351
00:13:52,310 --> 00:13:53,310
Keep all of your work

352
00:13:53,310 --> 00:13:54,630
at least six inches

353
00:13:54,630 --> 00:13:57,860
inside the hood.

354
00:13:57,860 --> 00:13:58,900
Don't use your hood

355
00:13:58,900 --> 00:14:00,380
as a storage cabinet.

356
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:01,980
Too many objects inside a hood

357
00:14:01,980 --> 00:14:05,820
can distort its airflow.

358
00:14:05,820 --> 00:14:06,500
Pay attention

359
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:07,820
to your airflow monitors

360
00:14:07,820 --> 00:14:09,380
and keep the hood sashed down

361
00:14:09,380 --> 00:14:10,420
as far as possible

362
00:14:10,420 --> 00:14:12,980
when you work.

363
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:14,460
Be sure your hood is checked

364
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:18,020
or recertified regularly.

365
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:19,300
And be prepared in case

366
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:24,210
of a hood-related emergency.

367
00:14:24,210 --> 00:14:25,490
Choosing the right hood

368
00:14:25,490 --> 00:14:26,250
for the job

369
00:14:26,250 --> 00:14:27,890
and working with it correctly

370
00:14:27,890 --> 00:14:29,450
will isolate the contaminants

371
00:14:29,450 --> 00:14:30,810
your work may generate

372
00:14:30,810 --> 00:14:31,810
and keep you

373
00:14:31,810 --> 00:14:34,450
and your coworkers safe.
